# 18. Stress Cool-down Trigger

The source-defined anomaly response is:

```text
Stress Cool-down Trigger
```

The term should remain exactly that unless another artifact defines the implementation.

It does not establish whether “cool-down” means:

- reduced reasoning depth;
- reduced interaction frequency;
- delayed task execution;
- user-interface pacing;
- breathing guidance;
- system suspension;
- communication changes;
- agent throttling;
- some other intervention.

Those are all implementation hypotheses.

The artifact only supports:

$$
ElectrodermalAnomaly
\rightarrow
StressCoolDownTrigger
$$

as an AMOS model route.

______________________________________________________________________

# 19. Relation to UBI × Emotion

The previously supplied UBI × Emotion artifacts define:

$$
\vec E_t
=
\langle
v_t,a_t,d_t
\rangle
$$

with:

$$
a_t
=
Arousal
$$

and a rule:

$$
a_t>0.90
\Rightarrow
ActivateCoolingCircuit.
$$

The NeuroSyncAI matrix now supplies:

$$
GSRAnomaly
\Rightarrow
StressCoolDownTrigger.
$$

This produces a strong conceptual correspondence:

$$
ElectrodermalStress
\sim
Arousal
$$

and:

$$
StressCoolDownTrigger
\sim
ActivateCoolingCircuit.
$$

But structural similarity cannot establish identity.

Therefore:

$$
StressCoolDownTrigger
\stackrel{?}{=}
ActivateCoolingCircuit
$$

remains unresolved.

Possible hypotheses:

### H1

`Stress Cool-down Trigger` invokes `ActivateCoolingCircuit`.

### H2

They are separate controls operating at different architectural planes.

### H3

The NeuroSyncAI trigger is a telemetry-level precursor and the UBI × Emotion cooling circuit is the downstream policy.

### H4

The names overlap but no canonical implementation relationship exists.

All remain:

$$
COMPETING
$$

until the binding resolves them.

