# 20. Three-Channel Control Topology

The matrix can be represented as:

```text
                UBI × NEUROSYNCAI
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          │            │            │
          ▼            ▼            ▼
         EEG           HRV          GSR
          │            │            │
          ▼            ▼            ▼
     GAMMA SYNC     VAGAL STATE   ELECTRODERMAL
          │            │            │
          ▼            ▼            ▼
    PhaseSync       RMSSD        TonicDrift
      > 0.65        > 35 ms      < 1.5 μS/min
          │            │            │
      anomaly        anomaly       anomaly
          │            │            │
          ▼            ▼            ▼
     FREQUENCY      COGNITIVE      STRESS
     RE-PACING      LOAD DOWN      COOL-DOWN
```

This is the simplest complete source-grounded routing graph.

______________________________________________________________________

# 21. Three Distinct Control Domains

The three channels appear to govern different aspects of the model:

| Channel   | Primary source-model concern         | Local action                 |
| --------- | ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------- |
| EEG Gamma | synchronization / phase coordination | Frequency Re-Pacing          |
| HRV       | biological/vagal pacing              | Cognitive Load De-escalation |
| GSR       | stress/arousal drift                 | Stress Cool-down Trigger     |

This suggests a three-axis adaptive governance model:

$$
\boxed{
Synchronization
+
Capacity
+
Stress
}
$$

or:

$$
\mathcal G_{NeuroSyncAI}
=
\langle
G_{sync},
G_{load},
G_{stress}
\rangle.
$$

That decomposition is **DERIVED** from the source row roles.

______________________________________________________________________

# 22. Multivariate State Representation

A normalized NeuroSyncAI telemetry state may be represented as:

$$
\mathbf N_t
=
\langle
\gamma_t,
h_t,
g_t
\rangle
$$

where:

$$
\gamma_t
=
GammaPhaseSync
$$

$$
h_t
=
HRV/RMSSDState
$$

$$
g_t
=
ElectrodermalTonicDrift.
$$

The source does not provide this vector notation; it is a derived normalization for RSCF storage.

The corresponding invariant vector is:

$$
\mathbf I
=
\left\langle
\gamma_t>0.65,
RMSSD_t>35ms,
Drift_t<1.5\mu S/min
\right\rangle.
$$

A conceptual validity function is:

$$
Valid_{NeuroSyncAI}
=
I_\gamma
\land
I_{HRV}
\land
I_{GSR}.
$$

However, the matrix never states that all three must simultaneously pass.

Therefore:

$$
I_\gamma\land I_{HRV}\land I_{GSR}
$$

should be treated as a **possible aggregate interpretation**, not source canon.

The matrix may support independent channel-local guards.

______________________________________________________________________

# 23. Locality Principle

The most direct reading is local:

$$
\neg I_\gamma
\Rightarrow
A_\gamma
$$

$$
\neg I_{HRV}
\Rightarrow
A_{HRV}
$$

$$
\neg I_{GSR}
\Rightarrow
A_{GSR}.
$$

This preserves one anomaly action per telemetry channel.

No source rule says:

$$
\neg I_\gamma
\Rightarrow
A_{HRV}
$$

or:

$$
\neg I_{GSR}
\Rightarrow
A_\gamma.
$$

Cross-channel escalation is therefore an unresolved layer.

______________________________________________________________________

# 24. Multi-Anomaly Cases

The matrix does not define what happens when multiple anomaly predicates activate simultaneously.

For example:

$$
\neg I_\gamma
\land
\neg I_{HRV}
$$

could produce:

```text
Frequency Re-Pacing
+
Cognitive Load De-escalation
```

but the matrix does not explicitly say whether actions:

- run concurrently;
- have precedence;
- compose;
- suppress one another;
- escalate to a stronger state;
- trigger a global safety veto.

Similarly:

$$
\neg I_\gamma
\land
\neg I_{HRV}
\land
\neg I_{GSR}
$$

has no defined arbitration rule in the matrix.

Therefore:

```yaml
multi_anomaly_arbitration:
  state: UNKNOWN_GAP
```

______________________________________________________________________

